Z||000000||Zorba||09-14-2000||03:15 AM||||How come these 16" wheels leave such big gaps<BR>inside the fender wells of this GT? You'd think the sizes are right, as follows:<BR>Dunlop SP SPORT 245/45/16 REAR and 225/45/16 FRONT<BR>   ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Zorba||09-14-2000||03:30 AM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lr.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Zorba||09-14-2000||03:39 AM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||09-14-2000||03:50 AM||||Although 45 series are a little thin for me tire wise, (The amount of tirewall between the rim and the tread of the tire.) Even when I went to 16s using 50 series tires I had a gap. Until you lower the car a little, make sure to clean the wheel wells because now everyone see them! Its just the way it is. 15 inch wheels will have almost the same gaps but with 60 series tires hides a little better.<P>Hope this helps!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Zorba||09-14-2000||04:04 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WKDFIRO:<BR><B>Although 45 series are a little thin for me tire wise, (The amount of tirewall between the rim and the tread of the tire.) Even when I went to 16s using 50 series tires I had a gap. Until you lower the car a little, make sure to clean the wheel wells because now everyone see them! Its just the way it is. 15 inch wheels will have almost the same gaps but with 60 series tires hides a little better.<P>Hope this helps!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes it does. Why size tires would be ideal for these 16" wheels to make the car look right? I think the contrast between the inside of the wheel wells and the pic angles are a little deceiving? May be not...they almost look like 13-14" wheels...don't they?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||AkursedX||09-14-2000||09:54 AM||||I believe the proper tire size for a 16 inch rim on a Fiero is the 50 series.  It could even be 55.  I use a 60 series on my stock 15 inch rims, and I know a 45 series is the same height for a 17 inch rim.  <BR>An easy trick is to go to tirerack and do a wheel search for a fiero and look up a 16 inch rim, and i think it should give you the proper tire size as well.<P>Although if you lower it just a little, it should be fine.  I like those rims too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ||||reg||3
Z||000006||Oreif||09-14-2000||10:43 AM||||You can go to bigger tires as suggested or just lower the car. My car has 225/50R15's on it and the gap is small and even.(may car is lowered 1") Going to bigger tires increases the over diameter of the tire. This causes the speedo to be off.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Oreif1_3.jpg">  <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/wheel_5.jpg"> <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 09-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Zorba||09-14-2000||10:52 AM||||Did you use 1" lowering springs in front?<BR>rear? Where did you get them and how much?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||09-14-2000||06:39 PM||||The wheelhouse gaps look larger because they are larger.<BR>Your front tires are 0.72 inches smaller across the diameter than the stock 205/60-15's, and your rear tires ore 0.48 inches smaller than the stock 215/60-15's.<P>If you really want the car to look right, I would advice taller tires. I am running 235/60-15's on front and 245/60-15's on back. My wheelhouse gaps are small and even at stock ride height, and they make the car look kickass.<BR>     Taller tires will put your speedometer off. My uncle and I whipped up an electronic gizmo to make my speedo adjustable.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Zorba||09-15-2000||01:10 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>The wheelhouse gaps look larger because  <BR> Taller tires will put your speedometer off. My uncle and I whipped up an electronic gizmo to make my speedo adjustable.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>What brand tires do you have on your car? I have a car with 235 60 15 in the rear and 205-60-15's front and wanted to dump the front ones, rotate the rear ones to the front and buy a set of 245-60-16's for the rear or taller if such thing exists.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||09-15-2000||02:01 AM||||I used Eibach springs all around. I got them from the Fiero Store as part of a suspension kit. (Eibach Springs, KYB shocks, KYB struts, KONI sway bars, and poly bushings)The kit was around $650. ||||reg||1
